        "text": "In both computational neuroscience and neuromorphic computing gradient estimators are known to have variance and bias, due to both algorithmic and hardware constraints (Lillicrap et al., 2016; Laborieux et al., 2021; M. Payvand et al., 2020a; Cramer Benjamin et al., 2022; Payeur et al., 2021; Lillicrap et al., 2020; Pozzi et al., 2018; Sacramento et al., 2018; Rubin et al., 2021; Roelfsema & Holtmaat, 2018; Murray, 2019; Bellec et al., 2020; Liu et al., 2021; Neftci et al., 2019; Roy et al., 2019; Huh & Sejnowski, 2018; Zenke & Ganguli, 2018; Zenke & Neftci, 2020). Using mathematical analysis and empirical verification we found that the impact of variance and bias on learning is determined by the network size, activation function, the gradient norm, and the loss Hessian, such that the amount of variance and bias that are permissible or even desirable depends on these properties. Though our empirical verification was done using feedforward ANNs our mathematical analysis was formulated to be as general as possible, and we believe that our assumptions are reasonable for most cases that computational neuroscientists and neuromorphic chip engineers face. Furthermore, we add a corollary to Lemma 2.1 and discuss extensions of our analysis to multi-step optimization settings in Appendix A (see Corollary A.2). Thus, our work can inform research in these areas by providing a guide for how much variance and bias relative to the gradient norm is reasonable for a given network. For example, researchers could analyze a learning algorithm to determine its variance and bias, and then use our analyses to guide decisions about their network design."
